            <h1>The Vanishing 'Kidneys': Why India’s Wetlands are Dissolving into Wastelands</h1>
            <h2 class="deck">From Bengaluru’s Bellandur to the East Kolkata Wetlands, rapid urbanization, unchecked encroachment, and systematic neglect are wiping out these vital ecosystems, leaving cities vulnerable to both drought and deluge.</h2>
            
            <div class="byline">By <strong>Special Correspondent</strong></div>
            <div class="timestamp">Updated: October 26, 2023 09:15 IST</div>
        </div>

        <div class="main-content">
            <div class="article-body">
                <p>Wetlands in India, often described as the 'kidneys' of the landscape for their ability to filter water and regulate hydrological cycles, are facing an unprecedented existential threat. Across the country, from the high-altitude glacial lakes of the Himalayas to the coastal mangroves of the Sundarbans, these crucial ecosystems are being systematically filled, polluted, and built over.</p>

                <p>Despite being a signatory to the Ramsar Convention and possessing a National Wetland Conservation Programme, India continues to lose its natural wetlands at an alarming rate. According to some environmental estimates, India has lost nearly 30 per cent of its natural wetlands in the last four decades due to urbanization, agricultural expansion, and pollution.</p>
                
                <div class="special-reporting">Ground Report: The Urban Collapse</div>

                <h3>The Bengaluru Example</h3>
                <p>Nowhere is this crisis more visible than in India's metropolitan hubs. Bengaluru, once known as the City of Lakes, had over 280 lakes in the 1960s. Today, fewer than 80 remain, and most are classified as sewage-fed. The infamous Bellandur Lake, which has frequently caught fire due to methane buildup from industrial effluents and untreated sewage, has become a global symbol of urban ecological collapse.</p>

