                <p>“We are treating wetlands as wastelands,” says Dr. Ramachandra T.V., a veteran researcher at the Indian Institute of Science (IISc). “When you build over the natural drainage basins, you are inviting both water scarcity and flooding. The wetlands provide essential ecosystem services, and their loss cannot be compensated by concrete infrastructure.”</p>

                <h3>Kolkata’s Natural Sewage Treatment in Peril</h3>
                <p>On the eastern coast, the East Kolkata Wetlands (EKW)—a Ramsar site of international importance—serves as the city's natural sewage treatment plant. A vast network of pisciculture ponds and agricultural fields, the EKW treats the city's sewage while providing livelihood to thousands.</p>

                <p>However, real estate pressure from the expanding city is eating into this delicate ecosystem. Conservationists warn that any significant damage to the EKW will necessitate the construction of billion-dollar mechanical sewage treatment plants, a cost the municipality can ill afford.</p>

                <div class="special-reporting">The Policy Gap</div>

                <h3>Legal Ambiguity and Implementation Failure</h3>
                <p>The primary hurdle in wetland conservation remains the lack of clear, actionable data and systemic implementation. While the Wetland (Conservation and Management) Rules, 2017 were meant to strengthen protection, they have been criticized for diluting central oversight and shifting the responsibility to states, many of which lack the resources or political will to enforce them.</p>

                <p> Furthermore, the definition of a "wetland" often causes legal ambiguity. Many smaller water bodies or seasonal wetlands are not officially recorded, leaving them vulnerable to encroachment.</p>

