                </div>

                <p>“Wetland management requires a multi-departmental approach,” explains K.S. Murali, an environmental policy expert. “Currently, the responsibility is fragmented between revenue, forest, fisheries, and urban development departments. We need a unified authority that recognizes wetlands as protected spaces, similar to forests.”</p>

                <h3>Looking Ahead: The Cost of Neglect</h3>
                <p>The consequences of wetland depletion go beyond the loss of biodiversity. Wetlands are critical for flood mitigation, carbon sequestration, and groundwater recharge. Their disappearance is making Indian cities less resilient to climate change.</p>

                <p>As Chennai, Hyderabad, and Bengaluru have demonstrated in recent years, the death of wetlands is directly linked to catastrophic flooding. The cost of reclaiming these spaces is exponentially higher than the cost of conserving them today. India’s environmental future may depend on how quickly it can shift its perspective from viewing wetlands as real estate to recognizing them as essential natural infrastructure.</p>

